Assuming the HTML has an image with the ID "my-image":

```
<img id="my-image" src="original-image.jpg">
```

The following jQuery script would change the image to "hover-image.jpg" when the user hovers over it, and change it back to the original image when the user moves their cursor away:

```
$(document).ready(function() {
  $('#my-image').hover(
    function() {
      $(this).attr('src', 'hover-image.jpg');
    },
    function() {
      $(this).attr('src', 'original-image.jpg');
    }
  );
});
``` 

This code waits for the document to be ready before attaching a hover event to the image with the ID "my-image". When the user hovers over the image, the jQuery `hover()` function's first argument executes, and it sets the `src` attribute of the image to "hover-image.jpg". When the user moves their cursor away from the image, the second argument of the `hover()` function executes, and it resets the `src` attribute to "original-image.jpg".

Yes, you can add a smooth transition effect when the image changes by using CSS property `transition` in combination with jQuery.

Here's the updated jQuery script that includes a smooth transition effect:

```
$(document).ready(function() {
  $('#my-image').hover(
    function() {
      $(this).css('opacity', '0.5').attr('src', 'hover-image.jpg');
      $(this).animate({
        opacity: 1
      }, 300);
    },
    function() {
      $(this).css('opacity', '0.5');
      $(this).attr('src', 'original-image.jpg');
      $(this).animate({
        opacity: 1
      }, 300);
    }
  );
});
```

This code smoothly transitions the image by using CSS `opacity` property, which can smoothly change from one value to another with the help of jQuery `animate()` function. The transition effect is set to `300` milliseconds. 

When the user hovers over the image, first it reduces alpha/transparency to `0.5` and then changes the `src` attribute to "hover-image.jpg". After that, it smoothly changes the alpha back to its original value (i.e., from `0.5` to `1`). 

And when the user's cursor moves away from the image, first it reduces alpha/transparency to `0.5`, then changes the `src` attribute to `original-image.jpg`, and finally smoothly increases the alpha back to `1`.

Certainly! To change the speed of the transition effect, you can adjust the duration parameter of the `animate()` function in the script. The duration specifies the time in milliseconds that the animation should take to complete.

Here's an example of how you can increase the duration of the transition effect to 500 milliseconds:

```
$(document).ready(function() {
  $('#my-image').hover(
    function() {
      $(this).css('opacity', '0.5').attr('src', 'hover-image.jpg');
      $(this).animate({
        opacity: 1
      }, 500);
    },
    function() {
      $(this).css('opacity', '0.5');
      $(this).attr('src', 'original-image.jpg');
      $(this).animate({
        opacity: 1
      }, 500);
    }
  );
});
```

In this code, the duration of the transition effect is set to 500 milliseconds, which is double the speed of the original transition effect.

You can adjust the duration parameter to any value you like, depending on how fast or slow you want the transition effect to be.

Sure thing! To add a fade-in and fade-out effect when the image changes, you can use the jQuery `fadeIn()` and `fadeOut()` methods.

Here's an updated version of the script that includes a fade-in and fade-out effect:

```
$(document).ready(function() {
  $('#my-image').hover(
    function() {
      $(this).css('opacity', '0.5').attr('src', 'hover-image.jpg');
      $(this).fadeOut(0).fadeIn(500);
    },
    function() {
      $(this).css('opacity', '0.5');
      $(this).attr('src', 'original-image.jpg');
      $(this).fadeOut(0).fadeIn(500);
    }
  );
});
```

In this code, the `fadeOut()` method is called with a duration of 0 milliseconds to quickly fade out the image. Next, the `fadeIn()` method is called with a duration of 500 milliseconds to fade in the new image with a smooth transition effect.

You can adjust the fade-in and fade-out durations to any value you like, depending on how fast or slow you want the effect to be.

